to knock you out.

1016
01:07:10,497 --> 01:07:11,699
[Malcolm] But you had
to go ahead

1017
01:07:11,799 --> 01:07:12,967
and eat the whole thing.

1018
01:07:13,066 --> 01:07:14,569
[unsettling music]

1019
01:07:14,669 --> 01:07:17,672
[Malcolm] You're... I should've
known you'd be different.

1020
01:07:21,509 --> 01:07:25,212
They present themselves
to you as women, do they?

1021
01:07:27,481 --> 01:07:29,617
[Malcolm] Because that
they are not.

1022
01:07:30,250 --> 01:07:34,589
[sighs] In truth, I--I--I
don't know what they are, um,

1023
01:07:34,689 --> 01:07:37,659
and I've known them a long time,
a long time.

1024
01:07:37,759 --> 01:07:39,827
Uh...

1025
01:07:40,360 --> 01:07:42,997
-Malcolm...
-[Malcolm] Yes, Liz?

1026
01:07:43,631 --> 01:07:46,333
[Liz] What the fuck
are you talking about?

1027
01:07:46,701 --> 01:07:49,804
[music becomes nostalgic]

1028
01:07:50,738 --> 01:07:52,840
[Malcolm] <i>Yeah, Darren and I,</i>
<i>we were--we were kids</i>

1029
01:07:52,941 --> 01:07:54,609
<i>when we found their mother...</i>

1030
01:07:56,044 --> 01:07:58,078
<i>in that creek you saw.</i>

1031
01:07:58,680 --> 01:08:01,716
[Malcolm] <i>That was</i>
<i>more than 200 years ago.</i>

1032
01:08:04,351 --> 01:08:07,354
[Malcolm] <i>But somehow,</i>
<i>you look exactly like her.</i>

1033
01:08:07,855 --> 01:08:09,356
[the woman vocalizes softly]

1034
01:08:09,456 --> 01:08:10,825
[Malcolm] <i>She was</i>
<i>a beautiful woman,</i>

1035
01:08:10,925 --> 01:08:12,794
<i>their mother.</i>

1036
01:08:13,427 --> 01:08:14,862
[Malcolm] <i>But she was</i>
<i>a trespasser</i>

1037
01:08:14,963 --> 01:08:16,531
<i>on my father's land.</i>

1038
01:08:16,631 --> 01:08:18,398
[the woman hums]

1039
01:08:20,200 --> 01:08:22,770
[music becomes tense]

1040
01:08:23,203 --> 01:08:24,939
[Malcolm] <i>We'd seen her</i>
<i>many times before,</i>

1041
01:08:25,039 --> 01:08:29,242
<i>dancing naked in the river</i>
<i>by the light of the moon.</i>

1042
01:08:29,944 --> 01:08:32,046
[the woman gasps]

1043
01:08:32,412 --> 01:08:33,881
[Malcolm] <i>And that</i>
<i>wasn't allowed.</i>

1044
01:08:35,016 --> 01:08:37,752
-[shot echoes]
-[the woman cries out]

1045
01:08:37,852 --> 01:08:39,654
[the woman howls in pain]

1046
01:08:39,754 --> 01:08:40,722
[music becomes nostalgic]

1047
01:08:40,822 --> 01:08:42,857
[howling continues]

1048
01:08:46,460 --> 01:08:47,762
[Malcolm] <i>We threw her</i>
<i>in with the swine...</i>

1049
01:08:47,862 --> 01:08:48,796
[boys laughing]

1050
01:08:48,896 --> 01:08:50,031
<i>...not caring about her</i>

1051
01:08:50,130 --> 01:08:51,766
<i>or whatever bastard</i>
<i>she was carrying.</i>

1052
01:08:51,866 --> 01:08:55,703
-[door shuts and latches]
-[the woman whimpers]

1053
01:08:56,671 --> 01:08:59,172
[swine snorting]

1054
01:08:59,272 --> 01:09:01,909
[the woman groans and grunts]

1055
01:09:05,880 --> 01:09:08,348
[groaning]

1056
01:09:10,718 --> 01:09:12,754
[agonized howling]

1057
01:09:15,288 --> 01:09:17,190
[howling stops abruptly]

1058
01:09:17,290 --> 01:09:19,493
[music fades out]

1059
01:09:37,945 --> 01:09:41,214
[Malcolm] <i>When we returned,</i>
<i>we saw she'd given birth.</i>

1060
01:09:41,314 --> 01:09:43,785
[wind whistling]

1061
01:09:43,885 --> 01:09:45,753
[Malcolm] <i>But to what?</i>

1062
01:09:45,853 --> 01:09:49,256
[wood creaking]

1063
